Gland
Mitsuru Yoshino, MD, Kenzo Hiroshima, MD,
Shinichiro Motohashi, MD, Kiyoshi Shibuya, MD,
Akira Iyoda, MD, Yasuo Sekine, MD, and
Takehiko Fujisawa, MD
Departments of Thoracic Surgery and Basic Pathology,
Graduate School of Medicine, Chiba University, Chuo-ku,
Chiba
In 1999, the World Health Organization introduced a new
classiﬁcation of a rare thymic cancer called papillary
adenocarcinoma of the thymus gland. In this study we
report on a case of papillary thymic carcinoma in a
29-year-old woman. Histologically, the tumor consisted
of a papillary component admixed with a solid compo-
nent, a component that is commonly found in type A
thymoma.
(Ann Thorac Surg 2005;80:741–2)
© 2005 by The Society of Thoracic Surgeons
V
arious tumors may originate in the thymus gland. In
1999, a new classiﬁcation of thymic cancer, papillary
thymic carcinoma, was introduced by the World Health
Organization [1]. This type of carcinoma is extremely rare
with only 5 patient cases reported in the literature [2, 3].
We report the case of a 29-year-old woman with the
clinical and pathologic features of papillary thymic car-
cinoma and we discuss the origin of the tumor.
A 29-year-old woman was found to have a cystic tumor in
the anterior mediastinum. She was asymptomatic and
her laboratory data were within normal limits. A roent-
genogram of the chest revealed a mass with regular
margins and a clear border. Computed tomography of
the chest revealed an anterior mediastinal tumor that
appeared to be encapsulated and had not invaded adja-
cent organs. The tumor, approximately 5.5 /H110035.0 /H110034.2 cm
was removed by total extended thymectomy and was
found to have an elastic, hard consistency.
Microscopically, the tumor cells were cuboidal or spin-
dle shaped with large, ovoid nuclei that had a ground-
glass appearance and contained pseudo inclusions and
nuclear grooves. The tumor consisted of papillary archi-
tecture and a solid component (Fig 1). Psammoma bodies
and follicles, similar to those seen in thyroid tumors were
observed. Although these histologic ﬁndings were con-
sistent with those of a papillary thyroid carcinoma, the
tumor had the feature of a solid growth of spindle or
ovoid cells and showed transition to the papillary com-
ponent. Foci with a storiform pattern of growth were also
present, a feature commonly seen in type A thymomas.
Mitosis was observed in both the spindle cell component
and in the papillary component. Although the tumor
invaded the capsules, it had not extended further. The
presence of Hassall’s corpuscle both inside and outside
the capsule indicated that the tumor had originated in
the thymus gland.
The results of immunohistochemical investigations are
summarized in Table 1 . Ber-EP4 was positive in the
papillary component and negative in the solid compo-
nent. The Ki-67 labeling index of the tumor cells was
2.9%. Clinical evaluation after surgery ruled out the
possibility of a primary tumor in other organs, such as
the thyroid gland or the ovary.
